<Base title="Your first setup, step by step" description="A plain-language Get Ready guide: open your coding agent, hand over setup, handle account sign-in and check a real HTTPS app. Includes Cloudflare and Namecheap instructions.">
<div class="guide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Get Ready · First steps</p><h1>You don’t need an app idea.</h1><p class="lede">Start with a place for your agent to work. It checks what’s missing and handles the technical setup. You sign in to your own accounts and approve changes that need your say.</p><a href="#first-step" class="button">Start here <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true"/></a></div>
<details class="guide-flow"><summary>See who does what</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width: 650px)" srcset="/diagrams/get-ready-mobile.svg"/><img src="/diagrams/get-ready.svg" width="1176" height="125" alt="You open your agent. The agent checks your environment. You handle required sign-in or approval. The agent deploys and verifies HTTPS."/></picture><figcaption>Who does what. Sign-in is only needed when an account check asks for it. <a href="/diagrams/get-ready.d2">Editable D2 source</a></figcaption></figure></details>
<div class="guide-steps">
<section id="first-step" class="guide-step"><div class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div><h2><Laptop size={24} aria-hidden="true"/>Open your coding agent.</h2><p>On your computer, open Codex and choose a project folder. An empty folder is fine. If you already have a project open, stay there.</p><details><summary>I don’t have Codex yet</summary><ol><li>Open the <a href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/app">official desktop app instructions <ExternalLink size={15} aria-hidden="true"/></a> and use the download for your computer.</li><li>Install the app, open it and sign in to your ChatGPT account.</li><li>Choose Codex mode, start a new chat and open or create a project folder. The app’s labels may differ by version.</li><li>Return here for the setup handoff in step 2.</li></ol><p class="note">The automated reference setup is currently verified on an Apple Silicon Mac. Other systems need separate agent-led verification. You don’t need to buy an API key to copy this handoff.</p></details></div></section>
<section class="guide-step"><div class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div><h2><Rocket size={24} aria-hidden="true"/>Give the agent the setup job.</h2><p>Copy this handoff, paste it into the Codex chat for that folder and send it. You can say “I don’t have a domain” or “I already use Cloudflare.” No project brief is needed.</p><ReadyHandoff/><p class="note">The handoff asks the agent to inspect your actual environment. This website cannot install tools or change your accounts by itself.</p><details><summary>What should the agent do next?</summary><p>Find the missing tools, check GitHub access and identify the correct Cloudflare account and destination. It should tell you the exact human step when one is needed, save progress privately and continue other checks. It must check again after a fix; yesterday’s green result isn’t enough.</p></details></div></section>
<section class="guide-step"><div class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div><h2><KeyRound size={24} aria-hidden="true"/>Sign in when the agent asks.</h2><p>Keep passwords, verification codes and secret keys in the official sign-in screen or your password manager. Never paste them into this website, a specification or a chat message.</p><details><summary>GitHub: where your app’s source lives</summary><ol><li>When the agent opens a GitHub login or device verification page, sign in to the account you want to own the app.</li><li>Review the requested permissions and confirm the account and repository with the agent.</li><li>Tell the agent you’ve finished. It checks access and continues.</li></ol><p>If you need an account, use <a href="https://github.com/signup">GitHub’s own signup</a>. You handle its terms and account verification. <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/about-authentication-to-github">GitHub authentication guide</a>.</p></details><details><summary>Cloudflare: where your app runs</summary><ol><li>Use <a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com/">Cloudflare’s dashboard</a> to sign in to your own account.</li><li>If you have more than one account, tell the agent which one owns the domain.</li><li>Complete any account verification on Cloudflare, then let the agent recheck access.</li></ol><p>You decide whether to accept a new subscription, billing commitment or permission. The agent should explain the cost before asking. An alert is not a hard spending cap.</p></details><details><summary>My domain is already on Cloudflare</summary><p>A domain is your web address, such as <code>example.com</code>. Tell the agent its name. It checks the account, current DNS records and the intended app address before changing anything. You normally do not need to change nameservers again.</p><p>Do not delete existing mail or website records to make a setup check pass. The agent must preserve them or explain a necessary change.</p></details><details><summary>My domain is at Namecheap</summary><p>Only do this when the agent has reviewed the current DNS records and you have agreed to move DNS to Cloudflare. Nameservers tell the internet which service holds your domain’s address book.</p><ol><li>Add the domain in Cloudflare using its <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/dns/zone-setups/full-setup/setup/">full setup guide</a>. Ask the agent to review the imported website and email records first.</li><li>Have the agent check <strong>DNSSEC</strong>, the protection that signs DNS records. If it is active, the agent must explain and ask you to approve the migration step: turn off the old DNSSEC configuration at the registrar and verify the old DS record is gone before changing nameservers. Leaving the old signature in place can make the domain unreachable.</li><li>Copy the two nameservers assigned to <em>your</em> domain by Cloudflare. Don’t copy nameservers from an example.</li><li>In Namecheap, open <strong>Domain List → Manage → Nameservers → Custom DNS</strong>. Enter those two nameservers and save using the checkmark.</li><li>Tell the agent you’ve saved them. It waits for Cloudflare to report the zone active, then checks DNS and your app’s HTTPS address. If DNSSEC was disabled for the move, follow the agent’s instructions to enable Cloudflare DNSSEC, add its new DS record at the registrar and verify it.</li></ol><p><a href="https://www.namecheap.com/support/knowledgebase/article.aspx/767/10/how-to-change-dns-for-a-domain/">Namecheap’s current instructions and screenshots</a> · <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/dns/nameservers/update-nameservers/">Cloudflare’s nameserver instructions</a></p><p class="note">If Cloudflare is your registrar, or DNS is already active there, skip this change. Other registrars use different screens; let the agent find their official instructions.</p></details><details><summary>I don’t have a domain</summary><p>Tell your agent. A domain purchase needs your approval; it should never buy one because a form suggests it. A temporary Cloudflare address is a possible deployment destination, but the current automated reference operation only supports an existing authorised Worker and route. Your agent must explain any separate setup needed.</p></details></div></section>
<section class="guide-step"><div class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">4</div><div><h2><Globe size={24} aria-hidden="true"/>Open your first live app.</h2><p>The agent should give you an HTTPS link to your reference app and evidence that it deployed the intended source. Open the link. The agent should also change the greeting and deploy again. Refresh and check the new greeting.</p><p>That proves more than a checklist: the agent can build, deploy and change the app you own. Only then move to <a href="/init">Init</a> to describe the app you want.</p><details><summary>Try the next step: a Hello app</summary><ol><li>Open <a href="/init">Init</a> and choose <strong>Hello World reference</strong>.</li><li>Write a title and introduction. Choose a theme.</li><li>For the currently supported operation, choose <strong>An existing domain</strong> and enter the exact hostname your agent just verified.</li><li>Use <strong>Save and preview</strong>, then <strong>Download spec</strong> and <strong>Copy to Codex</strong>.</li><li>Give the JSON file and handoff to the agent. It reviews the configuration, commits it, deploys through the app’s own release path and verifies the live result.</li></ol><p>The form saves only a local draft. A temporary address or new-domain choice explores intent; it does not execute that destination. <a href="/docs#hello-init">Supported Hello configuration details</a>.</p></details></div></section>
</div>
<section class="guide-support"><h2>If something stops.</h2><p>Tell the agent what the screen says, leaving out secrets. It should identify one specific action, such as finishing GitHub login, then resume from the saved progress. Provider outages and routine repairs belong to the agent.</p><p class="note">Working today: one reusable readiness engine, private resume and supported reference installation/configuration/deployment on the verified Mac path. New account/domain bootstrap, customer-fork deployment and hosted API/MCP still need completion. <a href="/release">Read the current evidence</a>.</p></section>
<section class="section guide-vision"><h2>The wider journey.</h2><p>Get Ready comes before an idea. Init captures your choices. Build, compose and own continue from there.</p><figure><a href="/guides/composer-vision-v1.webp"><img src="/guides/composer-vision-v1.webp" width="1536" height="1024" alt="Original App Composer concept: Get Ready, Init, Build, Compose and Own, with independent apps and a coding-agent workflow." loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption>Original concept supplied by the project owner. This describes the intended product, not a claim that every step is automated today. Open the image to read it at full size. <a href="/release">Verified features and remaining work</a>.</figcaption></figure></section>
<p class="small">Vendor instructions reviewed 13 October 2026. Official guides include the current provider screenshots; this page does not imitate account or sign-in screens.</p>
</Base>
